    "body": "Nelkarmi is one of two bounties from GSK’s $10.6 billion acquisition of Nuvalent Bio—the other being the ROS1 inhibitor zidesamtinib, which won FDA approval earlier this year as Jideytro.\nThe FDA has granted accelerated approval to GSK’s tyrosine kinase inhibitor neladalkib for ALK-positive non-small cell lung cancer, marking the second regulatory win from the pharma’s $10.6 billion takeover of Nuvalent Bio earlier this year.\nThe approval, which came 10 days ahead of the Nov. 27 target action date, allows the use of neladalkib for patients with advanced disease who have undergone prior lines of treatment for unresectable tumors. To keep the approval, GSK will need to confirm the drug’s clinical benefit in a confirmatory study. Neladalkib will be marketed under the brand name Nelkarmi, according to a Tuesday announcement .\nGSK acquired Nuvalent in June for $10.6 billion in one of the industry’s largest buys this year. The main bounties from the takeover were the biotech’s two lead non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) assets: the ROS1 inhibitor zidesamtinib, which the FDA approved in July as Jideytro, and Nelkarmi.\nAside from Jideytro and Nelkarmi, Nuvalent also brought GSK the HER2 blocker NVL-330, which is in Phase 1 development for NSCLC.\nMergers & acquisitions\nGSK pays $10.6B for cancer biotech Nuvalent in industry’s second-biggest buy of the year\nNuvalent Bio is GSK’s third big-ticket purchase this year, after the pharma dropped $2.2 billion in January for RAPT Therapeutics and $950 million in February for 35Pharma.\nJune 9, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more\nNelkarmi’s approval was supported by data from the Phase 1/2 ALKOVE-1 study, which enrolled 700 patients with ALK-positive solid tumors, GSK said on Tuesday . One of the trial’s primary outcomes is objective response rate (ORR). Nelkarmi resulted in a 49% ORR in patients whose only prior exposure to ALK-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKI) was through Genentech’s Alecensa. In patients who had received at least two prior lines of ALK TKI therapy, ORR was 32%.\nNelkarmi was designed to address drug resistance and treat NSCLC that has spread to the brain, GSK said in the Tuesday release. In ALKOVE-1, intracranial ORR hit 55% in patients with measurable metastasis in the central nervous system and whose only prior ALK TKI was Alecensa.\nNelkarmi had previously bagged the FDA’s breakthrough therapy designation, which is granted to therapies that address a serious disease and have shown early evidence of substantially improving outcomes versus available therapies.\nAs for tolerability, GSK noted that 5% of treated patients had to permanently discontinue Nelkarmi due to adverse reactions, the most common of which was liver toxicities. The drug’s prescribing information carries warnings and precautions for hepatotoxicity, renal toxicity, ocular toxicity, creatine phosphokinase elevation and embryo-fetal toxicity. Other common side effects include constipation, muscle or bone pain, edema and fatigue.\nGSK is running the Phase 3 ALKAZAR study for Nelkarmi in TKI-naive ALK-positive NSCLC patients and other solid tumors.\nApprovals\nGSK’s $10.6B Nuvalent bet pays off early as FDA approves lung cancer drug\nJideytro is GSK’s first approved product for lung cancer. With its takeover of Nuvalent Bio last month and a deal with Hansoh Pharmaceutical in 2023, the pharma now has several other lung cancer assets in late-stage development.\nJuly 23, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more",
